WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:07.558
A fraction is part of a whole.

2
00:00:09.533 --> 00:00:10.973
Here is one whole bar.

3
00:00:12.967 --> 00:00:15.103
Cut it into 4 equal parts.

4
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.679
Colour in 3 parts.

5
00:00:20.667 --> 00:00:22.571
We write this as three quarters.

6
00:00:24.567 --> 00:00:27.818
The bottom number: how many equal parts in total.

7
00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:32.540
The top number: how many parts we colour in.

8
00:00:34.533 --> 00:00:36.251
The parts MUST be equal.

9
00:00:38.233 --> 00:00:41.159
This bar has 4 parts, but they are not equal.

10
00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:45.362
So the shaded part is NOT one quarter.

11
00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:49.701
Different fractions can be the same amount.

12
00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:52.907
Half of the bar.

13
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:56.015
Two quarters.

14
00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.068
Four eighths.

15
00:01:01.067 --> 00:01:03.482
Look! The coloured part is the same length.

16
00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:06.557
These are called equivalent fractions.

17
00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:09.483
Same amount, different names.

18
00:01:11.467 --> 00:01:13.232
To make an equivalent fraction...

19
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:21.126
...multiply the top AND the bottom by the same number.

20
00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:24.865
We can also go the other way.

21
00:01:25.833 --> 00:01:27.551
This is called simplifying.

22
00:01:29.533 --> 00:01:32.227
Six eighths. Can we use fewer, bigger parts?

23
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.311
Find the HCF of 6 and 8. It is 2.

24
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.622
Divide the top and the bottom by 2.

25
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:49.340
Six eighths is the same as three quarters!

26
00:01:55.333 --> 00:01:58.212
Whatever you do to the top, do to the bottom!
